      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
          "content": "There are limited data on early and long-term prognosis in patients after aortic valve replacement who have left ventricular dysfunction, reduced ejection fraction (EF) < or =35% and no concomitant coronary artery disease."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To assess the prognosis in this group of patients depending on the mean aortic gradient (MAG) value."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "This study involved 60 patients with severe aortic stenosis and EF < or =35%. Patients with coronary artery disease, more than moderate aortic regurgitation and any other valvular lesion were excluded. Patients were divided into two groups based on the MAG values: group I included patients with MAG < or =35 mmHg, and group II included patients with MAG >35 mmHg."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Early mortality after aortic valve replacement was 14.2% in group I, and 5.1% in group II. During a mean follow-up of 48 months mortality in groups I and II was 16.6% and 2.6%, respectively. In the follow-up period, a significant functional improvement according to NYHA scale as well as significant decrease of left ventricular dimensions and increase of EF was observed in both groups of patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Patients with severe aortic stenosis, left ventricular ejection fraction <35% and MAG < or =35 mmHg constitute a group of the highest early and long-term mortality risk after valve replacement. In turn, patients with MAG >35 mmHg should be classified as the group of slightly increased risk."
        }
      ]},
